    "statement0": "The word <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"controlling\" </i></span> is mentioned in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">9.2%</span> of papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. This puts in the bottom <span style=\"color: rgba(237, 12, 24, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">22<sup>nd</sup></span> percentile of words.<br> <br>Among papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"controlling\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">29.1%</span> of reported p-values are 'fragile' - i.e., just barely falling under the standard significance threshold (.01 \u2264 p < .05). This rate is similar to median Psychology paper (median shown in charcoal); relative to other words, papers with <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"controlling\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">57<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages. Note, the strength of a paper's p-values strongly predict its replicability.",
    "statement1": "As of 2020-2024, papers containing controlling tend to be published in higher impact factor journals (83<sup>rd</sup> percentile among all words). Papers containing controlling also tend to be cited less (21<sup>st</sup> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that lower-ranked institutions are more likely to publish papers containing controlling (6<sup>th</sup> percentile). "
